Complete macOS Support for mbuffer
===================================

This patch provides full macOS compatibility for mbuffer, combining:
1. Removal of 2GB buffer size limitation (condition variables replace semaphores)
2. macOS-specific platform support (memory detection, fsync handling)
3. Compilation fixes for optional debugging modules (idev.so, tapetest.so)

PROBLEMS ADDRESSED:
-------------------
1. macOS doesn't implement sem_init() for unnamed POSIX semaphores (ENOSYS)
2. Buffer size limited to ~2GB due to 32-bit semaphore counters
3. configure script's objdump fails on macOS (Mach-O binary format)
4. Missing macOS memory detection APIs
5. fsync errors on APFS and other macOS filesystems

SOLUTION OVERVIEW:
------------------
- Replace POSIX semaphores with pthread condition variables + 64-bit counters
- Add macOS Mach VM API support for memory detection
- Handle macOS-specific fsync error codes (EOPNOTSUPP/ENOTSUP)
- Provide fallback C library symbol names for macOS (_open vs open, etc.)

IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS:
-----------------------
Core synchronization (removes 2GB limit + macOS semaphore dependency):
- globals.h/c: New condition variable sync primitives and 64-bit counters
- common.h/c: Counter operation functions (counter_init/wait/post/getvalue)
- mbuffer.c: Replaced all sem_* operations with counter_* operations
- input.c: Replaced all sem_* operations with counter_* operations
- settings.c: Removed semaphore limit validation checks

macOS-specific enhancements:
- mbuffer.c: Added Mach VM headers and host_statistics64() memory detection
- mbuffer.c: Added EOPNOTSUPP/ENOTSUP to fsync error handling
- idev.c: Conditional C library symbol definitions for macOS
- tapetest.c: Conditional C library symbol definitions for macOS

Documentation:
- README: Updated MacOS section (now supported!)
- README: Updated 64 Bit Buffers section (limitation removed)

BENEFITS:
---------
- Full macOS compatibility (compiles and runs on macOS)
- No buffer size limitations (supports multi-GB buffers)
- All components compile (mbuffer, idev.so, tapetest.so)
- No external dependencies beyond POSIX threads
- More portable (condition variables vs unnamed semaphores)
- Better cross-platform support

